YAML Basics for Ansible

Today I learned about YAML, the language used to write Ansible Playbooks.
 Before automating anything, understanding YAML structure is very important.
Think of YAML as the “grammar” of automation.

🔹 YAML Syntax
YAML uses simple key-value pairs:
key: value
It is:
 Human-readable
 Clean and structured
 Easy to understand
Everything in Ansible Playbooks is written using this format.

🔹 Indentation Rules
Indentation is the most important part of YAML.
Use spaces only (not tabs)
 Maintain consistent spacing (commonly 2 spaces)
 Wrong indentation = Playbook error
In YAML, structure depends completely on spacing.

🔹 Lists in YAML
Lists are written using -
Example:


packages:
 - nginx
 - git
 - curl

Lists are used when defining multiple items like packages, users, or servers.

🔹 Dictionaries in YAML
Dictionaries contain multiple key-value pairs under one parent key.
Example:

user:
 name: aditya
 shell: /bin/bash
 state: present

This helps organize structured configuration data.

🔹 Variables in YAML
Variables make Playbooks dynamic and reusable.
Example:

vars:
 package_name: nginx

These variables can be reused inside tasks using {{ variable_name }} syntax.
This makes automation flexible instead of hardcoded.

🔥 3-Line Takeaway
YAML defines structure.
 Indentation defines hierarchy.
 Clean YAML = Clean Automation.
